  "preamble": "Monophthongization of the diphthong *ei to a high front vowel [iː]. It took place directly within Proto-Germanic, where the remaining *ei became *ī in the late period (*teiwaz > *Tīwaz, PIE *stéygʰeti > *stīganą 'to climb'), and in Proto-Slavic, where Proto-Balto-Slavic *ei became *ī as part of the general monophthongization of diphthongs (*weiˀdēˀtei > *viděti 'to see'), while Baltic kept a diphthong (Lithuanian veizdėti, žiema). The same outcome in Latin (deicō > dīcō) and post-classical Greek (ει > [i]) passed through an attested intermediate [eː] stage and is therefore a chain, not a one-step change. Albanian monophthongized Proto-Albanian *ei to i (*leitšja > lis 'oak').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Monophthongization, Raising",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Proto-Germanic",
      "languageFamily": "Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "PIE *stéygʰeti 'climbs'",
          "to": "*stīganą 'to climb' (Gothic steigan, Old English stīgan)",
          "note": "PIE *ei became Proto-Germanic *ī; Greek στείχω keeps the diphthong."
        },
        {
          "from": "Early Proto-Germanic *teiwaz 'god' (PIE *deywós)",
          "to": "Late Proto-Germanic *Tīwaz",
          "note": "Late Proto-Germanic eliminated the remaining /ei/, changing it to /iː/."
        }
